WEBVTT


00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:17.040
Yoolim: We have a lot of different ways to
classify all sorts of things in the world.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.950
Chemists, for example, have a table
of elements that sorts the different

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:23.250
elements according to specific
properties, and biologists have a way

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.840
to classify all the different species
of animals based on shared traits.

00:00:27.509 --> 00:00:30.930
With these classifications, we can
work out key phenomena in biological

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:34.710
evolution or make predictions on how
these different chemicals might react.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.860
We can even consider something
as everyday as a deck of cards.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.430
Each card is classified according
to number, color, and suit or road

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.970
signs, which can also be organized
according to a neat scheme.

00:00:44.475 --> 00:00:46.605
Even Pokemon have a way
of being classified.

00:00:46.665 --> 00:00:49.964
We can use different traits and skills
to determine how they might fare

00:00:49.964 --> 00:00:51.525
and battle against other Pokemon.

00:00:51.585 --> 00:00:54.974
So whether we realize it or not, most
of the things that we know and enjoy,

00:00:55.004 --> 00:00:58.605
or perhaps even take for granted, have
been organized in an orderly way for us.

00:00:58.905 --> 00:01:02.114
But one thing that has yet to be
classified in such a way and is quite

00:01:02.114 --> 00:01:04.545
crucial to our day-to-day life is letters.

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:08.235
Good classifications are useful
to us because they tell us

00:01:08.235 --> 00:01:09.285
something about the world.

00:01:09.405 --> 00:01:12.655
The periodic table tells us about the
internal structure of chemicals elements.

00:01:12.995 --> 00:01:16.210
The classification of different species
tells us about biological origins,

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.750
something as graphic or visual like
road signs or deck of cards can tell

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:23.560
us about how different symbols can
combine to create meaning. Just as well

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.770
we have a lot of different scripts that
use a lot of different shapes, but we

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.560
haven't quite worked out how letters
are generated using these diverse

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.170
shapes with such a classification.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:34.360
We can work out and begin
to understand this question.

00:01:35.535 --> 00:01:38.205
My name is Yoolim Kim and I'm
a researcher at the Max Planck

00:01:38.205 --> 00:01:41.205
Institute for the science of human
history here in Jena Germany.

00:01:41.235 --> 00:01:44.205
And I'm a part of the Minds and
Traditions research group, which works

00:01:44.205 --> 00:01:46.035
on understanding graphic communication.

00:01:46.485 --> 00:01:49.725
And one of our key research programs
is understanding and quantifying the

00:01:49.725 --> 00:01:51.375
visual properties of letter shapes.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:01.950
Olivier: Our research group studies
how people use images to communicate.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.013
We study all sorts of graphic
codes from coin designs to

00:02:06.013 --> 00:02:08.353
pictographs, to of course writing.

00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:12.673
One way to look at writing
systems is to see them as codes

00:02:13.353 --> 00:02:18.443
that associate sounds with images,
that is with letters, and letter

00:02:18.443 --> 00:02:20.998
shapes is what this project is about.

00:02:21.358 --> 00:02:25.048
Looking at most letters, you can
see that they are composed of a

00:02:25.048 --> 00:02:27.298
number of smaller, basic shapes.

00:02:27.778 --> 00:02:31.948
The inventors of visual symbols
used these basic shapes like

00:02:32.008 --> 00:02:37.048
lego blocks, combining them to
create new and distinct shapes.

00:02:37.478 --> 00:02:40.063
That is the way that most
writing systems work.

00:02:40.063 --> 00:02:43.813
And you can see that for instance,
in the Latin alphabet, the one that's

00:02:43.813 --> 00:02:47.953
used to write German or English or
Vietnamese. Think about the tiny arch

00:02:48.013 --> 00:02:51.913
in N, it is also repeated in H and in M.

00:02:53.193 --> 00:02:54.943
Each script behaves differently.

00:02:54.973 --> 00:02:59.533
Some will have fewer building blocks
while others will have far a more

00:02:59.533 --> 00:03:02.383
extensive repertoire of basic shapes.

00:03:02.773 --> 00:03:07.903
Those with fewer building blocks
can use recombination to create

00:03:07.963 --> 00:03:09.903
new and distinctive letters shapes.

00:03:10.733 --> 00:03:12.913
Those that have far more building blocks

00:03:13.063 --> 00:03:15.673
don't need to use recombination that much.

00:03:15.883 --> 00:03:19.543
Some may use some individual
components only once or twice

00:03:19.663 --> 00:03:20.683
within the whole system.

00:03:21.403 --> 00:03:23.173
As a result of these differences

00:03:23.383 --> 00:03:28.333
some scripts will have very
distinctive letters and others won't.

00:03:28.453 --> 00:03:33.943
See these two scripts: letter shapes
in this writing system seem less

00:03:33.943 --> 00:03:35.593
diverse than the letters in that one.

00:03:36.358 --> 00:03:41.158
If you try to imagine what the
next letter in this first strip

00:03:41.218 --> 00:03:43.378
might be like, it isn't that hard.

00:03:44.038 --> 00:03:45.868
See, not surprising.

00:03:46.768 --> 00:03:48.748
These letters are predictable.

00:03:49.288 --> 00:03:52.408
They combine a small range of
shapes in a rather regular way.

00:03:52.798 --> 00:03:54.478
Not so much for the other script.

00:03:55.618 --> 00:03:59.128
That letter shape was hard to
predict just by looking at the rest.

00:03:59.698 --> 00:04:03.158
We start from intuitions like this,
but we want to go much further.

00:04:03.998 --> 00:04:08.248
Our attempt is to measure the
distinctiveness of letters in

00:04:08.248 --> 00:04:09.688
the world's writing systems.

00:04:10.078 --> 00:04:14.608
And we define distinctiveness
as the optimal exploration of

00:04:14.608 --> 00:04:16.438
the space of possible shapes.

00:04:16.678 --> 00:04:18.928
Yoolim: We look at written letters
as if they're just combinations

00:04:18.928 --> 00:04:20.188
of smaller building blocks.

00:04:20.398 --> 00:04:23.428
Once we know the building blocks of a
writing system and how they combined, we

00:04:23.428 --> 00:04:26.698
can begin to imagine letters that don't
quite exist in the system, but have the

00:04:26.698 --> 00:04:30.748
potential to do so because they use the
same building blocks as actual letters

00:04:30.778 --> 00:04:32.628
and combine according to the same rules.

00:04:32.858 --> 00:04:36.358
We call this a space of possible letter
shapes. With this information, we can

00:04:36.358 --> 00:04:39.568
begin to work out to what extent scripts
are making efficient use of their

00:04:39.568 --> 00:04:43.708
building blocks to generate letters that
are numerous, but also – importantly – as

00:04:43.708 --> 00:04:45.478
distinct from one another as possible.

00:04:45.838 --> 00:04:47.098
So how are we doing this?

00:04:47.458 --> 00:04:50.818
We've built a browser based application
called Glyph, which invites users

00:04:50.818 --> 00:04:54.178
to play a simple and fun game with
all sorts of different scripts.

00:04:54.478 --> 00:04:57.848
The game is built on a simple
premise that unfolds in two parts.

00:04:58.978 --> 00:05:02.218
The premise is that players are presented
with all the letters of the script and

00:05:02.218 --> 00:05:05.968
asked to select characters according to
a description or a rule of their choice.

00:05:06.478 --> 00:05:10.888
As an example, let's imagine all the
letters of the Greek alphabet. In the

00:05:10.888 --> 00:05:14.308
first part we look to see how we can
select characters according to whether or

00:05:14.308 --> 00:05:16.108
not they share a certain characteristic.

00:05:16.708 --> 00:05:17.578
Looking at the letters

00:05:17.578 --> 00:05:19.738
I can see that many of
them have rounded edges.

00:05:20.108 --> 00:05:23.173
I select the characters that do
and provide a brief description of

00:05:23.173 --> 00:05:25.003
the rule, motivating my selection.

00:05:25.633 --> 00:05:29.353
It's important that we select characters
in a way that makes sense to us.

00:05:29.893 --> 00:05:31.603
This will matter in the
second part of the game.

00:05:31.663 --> 00:05:34.633
In the second part, you can see that
I'm presented with the description I

00:05:34.633 --> 00:05:38.533
provided earlier and the same letters,
but in an order different to how I

00:05:38.533 --> 00:05:42.973
saw the letters previously. After
a small delay, all I have to do is

00:05:42.973 --> 00:05:44.743
select the same letters as before.

00:05:45.793 --> 00:05:45.943
Now

00:05:45.943 --> 00:05:49.533
you can see why it's important that the
rules are sensical and meaningful to you.

00:05:50.643 --> 00:05:53.668
Because I selected seven
characters based on my rule on

00:05:53.668 --> 00:05:55.828
roundedness, I earned seven points.

00:05:56.578 --> 00:06:00.688
The more rules you make, the more points
you earn, and the closer you are to being

00:06:00.688 --> 00:06:05.278
the top ranked among players from all
around the world. You can make as many

00:06:05.278 --> 00:06:09.028
rules as you'd like, as long as you can
think of novel ways to select characters.

00:06:10.618 --> 00:06:15.818
So you earn as many points as the number
of characters described by your rule and

00:06:15.818 --> 00:06:20.338
the more points you earn across all the
scripts, the more points you win overall.

00:06:21.538 --> 00:06:24.688
Glyph features more than 40 different
scripts, allowing players to create

00:06:24.688 --> 00:06:26.308
rules for a diverse set of letters.

00:06:26.548 --> 00:06:30.018
With this research, we are one step
closer to a visual grammar of letters.

00:06:30.798 --> 00:06:32.203
Olivier: Thanks to your participation

00:06:32.473 --> 00:06:36.133
we can take the first steps in
describing the building blocks

00:06:36.223 --> 00:06:58.593
of the world’s writing systems.